{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 RCA — mechanism proven; classified `incomplete-implementation` (cited); ESCALATED on AC3 design decision\n\nInvestigated under /rebar-debug in a fresh worktree off `origin/main`\n(`565e1856bc9d180876fdbcaa336ccd57ceb8202e`; the 2337 fix `83edf99` is an ancestor).\nStep 0: no duplicate ticket; sole report. Reported-against floor = current `origin/main`.\n\n### Proven mechanism (the guards are genuinely ABSENT — an omission, not broken behaviour)\n\n- **AC1/AC2 (mint-time warning) — absent.** `sign_manifest` → `_sign_manifest_under_lock` →\n  `_prepare_manifest_event` → `mint_opcert_record` (`src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py:293`) resolves\n  the signing key via `ensure_opcert_key`/`_opcert_own_key_paths` and mints, but never invokes the\n  verify-side resolver `_opcert_own_public_keys` to check whether a public counterpart is\n  resolvable. Confirmed statically: `_opcert_own_public_keys` has exactly ONE caller —\n  `verify_opcert_record` (`_opcert_signing.py:550`) — and none on the mint path. So no warning is\n  emitted when the public half is unresolvable.\n- **AC3 (startup/deploy health check) — absent.** No public-counterpart / `_opcert_own_*` /\n  `_read_opcert_pub` check exists anywhere in `mcp_server.py`, `_mcp_health.py`, or\n  `_opcert_binding.py` (grep: zero hits). `compose_startup_opcert_binding` (`mcp_server.py:717`)\n  validates only the PRIVATE key (present / 0600 / Ed25519 / not-encrypted, via `compose_signer`)\n  and aborts startup on an unusable signer — it never checks that a public counterpart is\n  resolvable.\n\nThese are `static` (content) hypotheses, confirmed by source + reference-set inspection; there is\nno runtime failure to reproduce because the whole defect is a SILENCE.\n\n### Legitimacy: `incomplete-implementation` (cited), NOT a fresh feature request\n\nDesign authority establishing these guards were *meant to exist*:\n- Parent bug `d26a-8ffa-97cd-4b2a` (`dire-expectable-terrapin`), closed `plan_defect`, explicitly\n  scoped **AC5 (mint-time WARNING) + AC6 (deploy/startup health check)** and SPLIT them into this\n  ticket as \"additive guards against the class regressing.\" That parent plan passed plan-review.\n- Designed-but-unwired seams already in-repo: the verify-side `_opcert_own_public_keys` resolver\n  (AC1), the `run_startup_store_sweep` startup-warning idiom (AC1), and the fail-closed startup\n  validator `compose_startup_opcert_binding` (AC3).\n\nSo approval is NOT the open question → not a `feature-request`, not `intended-behavior`. This is a\ndeferred, scoped omission = `incomplete-implementation`.\n\n**Attribution (scope-of-work).** This is an omission, so there is no introducing commit that\n\"broke\" it. By scope-of-work it attributes to `d26a-8ffa-97cd-4b2a` — the ticket whose acceptance\ncriteria (AC5/AC6) the missing behaviour falls under, which shipped the key-resolution fix without\nthese deferred guards.\n\n### Why I am ESCALATING rather than auto-fixing (per operator instruction)\n\nThe mint-time warning (AC1/AC2) is cleanly auto-fixable: purely additive, non-refusing, fully\npinned by the cited authority (warn, naming the resolved key path + principal; do not refuse; the\nexisting success path and returned signature are unchanged). Mirrors `run_startup_store_sweep`.\n\n**AC3 is NOT pinned by the cited authority** — it \"requires a design decision\" and \"alters\ndeploy-time behaviour,\" which the operator reserved for themselves. AC3 says the health check\n\"FAILS,\" but the repo contains TWO deliberate, conflicting precedents:\n\n- **(a) Hard-abort startup** — extend the existing fail-closed `compose_startup_opcert_binding`\n  (which already aborts on an unusable signer). Scoped to the on-box signing deploy only (both\n  `REBAR_OPCERT_ENV_ID` + `REBAR_IDENTITY_SIGNING_KEY` set); dev/stdio/genesis returns `None`\n  early and is unaffected; a correctly-configured deploy (post-2337, which derives the public line\n  in-process) still passes. Only a misconfigured signing deploy newly refuses to serve.\n- **(b) Report a degraded field, never abort** — mirror `store_status` / `/health` +\n  blue-green readiness gate. `_mcp_health.py` documents the explicit invariant: \"a health probe\n  that can fail is worse than one that reports a degraded field,\" and `run_startup_store_sweep` is\n  log-and-continue (\"a missing store must not abort boot\"; only `RemovedInputError` fails hard).\n\nThese behave materially differently on a misconfigured deploy (refuse-to-serve vs serve-degraded),\nand the choice is not resolved by the cited authority. Per the operating instruction to escalate\ndesign decisions / behaviour changes, I am handing this call to the operator.\n\n**Recommendation:** proceed on AC1/AC2 as specified; for AC3 adopt **option (a)** (extend\n`compose_startup_opcert_binding`, gated on the on-box signing deploy) — it is the closest existing\nprecedent (\"an unusable signer aborts startup\"), fail-closed matches the parent bug's whole point\n(silently-uncertifiable attestations are worse than a refused boot), and it has zero blast radius\non dev/stdio and on correctly-configured deploys. If the operator prefers non-aborting deploy\nposture, option (b) is the alternative. Awaiting direction before Phase 2 / any Gerrit push.\nNo `--force` or gate bypass was used; no code change pushed."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787961399818854001,"uuid":"61f7aeef-a137-4650-8726-c4c7510f6fcf"}